  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 236 PM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nWith mid-level ridging taking up residence across the western half \nof the country, the Southeast is poised to enter a regime of \nnorthwesterly flow that lingers through the entirety of the extended \nrange. Initially, this will drive a lower PWAT airmass into the area \nthat may provide some relief from the frontally-forced doldrums \nwe've been in over the past several days. However, a pattern \ncharacterized by deep, persistent northwesterly flow sets the stage \nfor diurnally-enhanced convection forced by ridge riders -- any \ndisturbances rounding the outer edge of the governing ridging. Given \ndrier mid-levels (and thus steeper lapse rates), any features sent \nsouthward along the eastern edge of the ridge would serve to locally \norganize convection, potentially supporting (at least) daily \nmarginal severe concerns if MCSs are able to be maintained, driven \nprimarily by wind/downburst risks as dry air is entrained. Timing \nand intensity will be heavily dependent upon the aforementioned \nfeatures meandering through mid-level flow. That being said, would \nnot be surprised to see an uptick in PoPs for Monday and Tuesday \nafternoon/evening as we enter the purview of HiRes guidance, which \nis better suited to attempt to resolve any mesoscale precipitation \nfeatures. \n\nHighs will be in the 90s for most through the long term, with heat \nindex values surging back into the triple digits. Cannot rule out \nthe necessity of a Heat product(s) (likely an Advisory) by midweek \nas moisture rebounds in tandem with heat peaking.",
